<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine of Preslav</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 900</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> was a Bulgarian writer and churchman of the late 9th and early 10th centuries, a pupil of Methodius and one of the leading figures of the Preslav literary school under Simeon I. He composed the Didactic Gospel, a cycle of sermons, and the Alphabetical Prayer, one of the earliest poems in Slavonic. He later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Preslav.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Evidence about Constantine's life is scarce, but he is believed to have been a disciple of Saint Methodius. After Methodius died in 885, the Germanic clergy in Great Moravia jailed Constantine and sold him as a slave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and around 886 moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, where he worked at the Preslav Literary School at the end of the 9th century and the beginning of the 10th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Didactic Gospel is usually dated to the first years of the reign of Tsar Simeon I, 893–894. A compilation of lectures on a number of church holidays, it is the first systematic work of sermons in Slavic literature, and it opens with the poetic preface known as the Alphabet Prayer. In 894 Constantine wrote the Histories, the first historical chronicle in Slavic literature. In 906, commissioned by Simeon I, he translated the Four Epistles against the Arians of Saint Athanasius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in response to the heresies then beginning to spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><p>He is also said to be the author of the Service for Methodius, which tells of Methodius's struggle for the recognition of Old Church Slavonic, and of the Proclamation of the Holy Gospels, which rejects admiration for the foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and champions Old Bulgarian as the means of developing and raising Bulgarian culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Constantine was one of the most prolific writers in Old Bulgarian and one of the leading scholars, writers and translators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> school. None of his original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survived the burning of Preslav by the Byzantine emperor John I Tzimisces in 972 and the period of Ottoman rule from 1396 to 1878. All of his works are known from later copies, the earliest of which date from the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine of Preslav” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Simeon I's reign brought a golden age of Slavonic letters: John the Exarch, <b>Constantine of Preslav</b> and Chernorizets Hrabar, author of the treatise On the Letters, translated and composed theological, historical and scientific works.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…dissolved, the leading writers worked in Bulgaria at the end of the 9th and the start of the 10th century: Clement of Ohrid, who had also worked in Moravia and most likely founded the Ohrid school, <b>Constantine of Preslav</b>, Chernorizets Hrabar and John Exarch, with Naum of Preslav.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Veliki Preslav</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Veliki Preslav</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.16,26.81&amp;year=900" aria-label="Veliki Preslav on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 632 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine of Preslav"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 902h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 902h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.16,26.81&amp;year=900">What was here in 900 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
